    "q-159965": {
      "dom_id": "step-q159965",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent Bernoulli random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ),\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample?",
      "worked_solution": "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\theta }+\\frac{2}{1-\\theta }\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\theta }+\\frac{2}{\\theta -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{\\theta }+\\frac{2}{\\theta -1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{6(\\theta -1)+2\\theta }{\\theta (\\theta -1)} & =0 \\\\ 6(\\theta -1)+2\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 8\\theta -6 & =0 \\\\ 8\\theta  & =6 \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}=\\frac{3}{4}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
