    "q-159971": {
      "dom_id": "step-q159971",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent Poisson random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-5\\theta +4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample?",
      "worked_solution": "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(-5\\theta +4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =-5+\\frac{4}{\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5+\\frac{4}{\\theta } & =0 \\\\ \\frac{-5\\theta +4}{\\theta } & =0 \\\\ -5\\theta +4 & =0 \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}=\\frac{4}{5}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
