    "q-159942": {
      "dom_id": "step-q159942",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent normal random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-3.54+2.2\\text{ }\\,\\theta -3\\text{ }\\,{\\theta }^{2},\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-3.54+2.2\\text{ }\\,\\theta -3\\text{ }\\,{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =2.2-6\\text{ }\\,\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.2-6\\text{ }\\,\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 6\\text{ }\\,\\theta  & =2.2 \\\\ \\theta  & ≈0.367\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}≈0.367,$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
