    "q-159939": {
      "dom_id": "step-q159939",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent exponential random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -15.8\\text{ }\\,\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -15.8\\text{ }\\,\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\theta }-15.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\theta }-15.8 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\theta } & =15.8 \\\\ 5 & =15.8\\text{ }\\,\\theta  \\\\ \\theta  & ≈0.316\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}≈0.316,$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
